<h2 id="warm-recheck">Warm re-check &mdash; fastest repeat-check</h2>

<p>
  The table above is cold. This one is its opposite: each tool at its
  <strong>fastest achievable repeat-check</strong> of an unchanged file. Only two tools
  here keep a real cross-run result cache, so only two have a genuinely faster warm number
  &mdash; <strong>Basilisk</strong> (its <code>--cache</code> result-cache) and
  <strong>mypy</strong> (its incremental <code>.mypy_cache</code>). Those columns are tagged
  <em>(warm)</em>.
</p>

<p>
  Pyright, ty and Pyrefly keep <strong>no</strong> cross-run result cache &mdash; they write no
  cache artifacts, expose no cache flag, and a repeat run is no faster than the first beyond
  process/OS warm-up &mdash; and zuban&rsquo;s mypy mode only reuses a <code>.mypy_cache</code>
  that measures the same as cold on these single-file checks. For those tools the honest best
  case <em>is</em> their cold time, shown here tagged <em>(no cache)</em> rather than a fabricated
  warm figure. The ✓ still marks the fastest cell, so you can see that Basilisk&rsquo;s warm
  re-check leads even against every other tool&rsquo;s best case.
</p>

